import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "Write a typed value as JSON text. Export name and retries from settings, with newlines and quotes in the name escaped into valid JSON.",
  taskTitle: "Write settings as JSON text",
  purpose: "Turn notification settings already held by the application into JSON text suitable for saving or sending. Write both an ordinary name and a name containing a newline and double quotes, checking that each has valid JSON spelling.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript writeSettings constructs an output object with name followed by retries, then calls JSON.stringify. JSON.stringify handles escaping quotes and newlines. The inputs here are settings created in the program, so this example does not validate external unknown data.",
  benefit: "encodeString combines the selected type's JSON conversion with writing text. For this Settings, it uses the declared fields and their order. You do not need to assemble JSON by manually joining quote characters.",
  setup: "import * as json from \"std/json\" makes the module available as json. struct Settings defines a named type; name: String and retries: Int specify its field types. deriving JsonEncode asks the compiler to supply conversion from this type to JSON.",
  after: [
    "Settings { name: \"Mio\", retries: 2 } constructs a value of that type. Pass it after json.encodeString to receive a String. Arguments follow a function name, separated by spaces; parentheses group inner expressions and calls. println receives the completed string.",
    "The second name contains an actual newline and double quotes. In the source, \\n represents the newline and \\\" represents a quote. encodeString writes JSON escapes such as \\n and \\\" in its output, so the newline inside the name does not split the displayed JSON across lines.",
    "pub effect fn main is the output entry point. -> Unit means no useful return value; with Console fails ConsoleError declares console use and possible output failure. It is not a declaration that JSON conversion fails.",
    "do runs the two println calls in order. The first _ <- discards the print result before continuing. encodeString only returns text; it does not save a file or send an API request.",
  ],
  details: [
    "A in the declaration encodeString<A> is the input value's type. where JsonEncode<A> requires JSON conversion support for that type. Here, deriving JsonEncode supplies it for Settings, and String and Int also have the necessary support.",
    "This named Settings writes name followed by retries in declaration order. It does not sort arbitrary fields alphabetically. The comparison covers the example's strings and small integers; it does not claim the same encoding for every TypeScript number or arbitrary runtime value.",
    "The result is String, not Json. Use encodeJson if you need an intermediate Json value, or json.stringify to write a Json you already have. Writing JSON does not itself check application rules such as requiring a nonempty name.",
  ],
}
